        ,
          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        },
        {
          "param": "m",
          "low": 2,
          "high": 8
        ,
          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, drastically cutting FF count versus full pipelining while still delivering 1 result/cycle. This is the primary area-optimization lever for this spec. With m=2..8, the optimizer can trade a small latency increase (ceil(N/m)+2 vs N+2) against large FF savings. The same data_width/n_iter/guard ranges apply since accuracy is identical across families. This family is most likely to produce the Pareto-optimal designs for min luts_plus_ffs under the throughput and accuracy constraints."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands >=1 MSPS throughput and max_abs_err <= 2^-10, with area (luts_plus_ffs) as the primary objective. Both pipelined and pipelined_m deliver 1 result/cycle, so throughput = Fmax, which easily exceeds 1 MSPS for any reasonable Fmax. The key trade-off is area vs accuracy: data_width and n_iter control accuracy, while the pipeline structure (full vs every-m-stages) controls FF count. I split the budget 50/50 between the two families. For pipelined, the search explores the accuracy boundary at minimal width/iterations. For pipelined_m, the additional m parameter lets the optimizer find the sweet spot where FF savings outweigh any Fmax or latency penalty. Ranges are kept tight (data_width 12-16, n_iter 10-16) because the 2^-10 error spec is modest; wider data or more iterations would waste area without improving the objective. angle_guard and frac_guard are bounded small to keep area low while giving the optimizer room to find the minimal sufficient guard."
}

